= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For the <partition function> $Z=\sum_{\{S\}}e^{-\beta E}$, differentiation with respect to the external field gives
$$
\frac{\partial\log Z}{\partial B}
=\frac1Z\sum_{\{S\}}\beta\!\left(\sum_iS_i\right)e^{-\beta E}
=\beta\sum_i\langle S_i\rangle.
$$
Therefore the <magnetization> per site is
$$
\boxed{m=\frac1{N\beta}\frac{\partial\log Z}{\partial B}}.
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <mean-field approximation> replaces
$$
S_iS_j=m(S_i+S_j)-m^2+(S_i-m)(S_j-m)
$$
by its first three terms. Since every site has $q$ neighbours,
$$
\sum_{\langle ij\rangle}S_iS_j\simeq qm\sum_iS_i-\frac{Nq}{2}m^2.
$$
The sites then decouple, and their three possible weights sum to
$$
1+e^{-\beta g+\beta(Jqm+B)}+e^{-\beta g-\beta(Jqm+B)}
=1+2\kappa\cosh\!\bigl(\beta(Jqm+B)\bigr).
$$
Hence $c_1=1$, $c_2=2$, and
$$
Z=e^{-\beta NJqm^2/2}
\left[1+2\kappa\cosh\!\bigl(\beta(Jqm+B)\bigr)\right]^N.
$$

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Using the field derivative while imposing the <self-consistency equation> gives
$$
\boxed{m=\frac{2\kappa\sinh[\beta(Jqm+B)]}
{1+2\kappa\cosh[\beta(Jqm+B)]}}.
$$
At $B=0$, $m=0$ is always a solution. Linearizing the right-hand side for small $m$ gives slope
$$
\frac{2\kappa\beta Jq}{1+2\kappa}.
$$
A continuous <phase transition> occurs when this slope crosses one, so, with $k_B=1$ and $\kappa_c=e^{-g/T_c}$,
$$
\boxed{1=\frac{2e^{-g/T_c}}{1+2e^{-g/T_c}}\frac{Jq}{T_c}}.
$$
On the side where the slope exceeds one, the nonzero solutions lower the mean-field free energy and the trivial solution is unstable. The equation has a physical continuous-transition solution only in the corresponding parameter range of this spin-one model.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

<Landau-Ginzburg theory> assumes a local <gradient expansion> for a slowly varying <order parameter>. At zero field the microscopic spin-flip symmetry sends $m\mapsto-m$, so only even powers occur. <Rotational symmetry> makes the leading derivative term $(\nabla m)^2$, while locality and analyticity near the transition organize higher powers and derivatives. Stability requires a positive gradient coefficient and, at an ordinary continuous transition, $\alpha_4(T_c)>0$; the coefficient $\alpha_2(T)$ changes sign at $T_c$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Write $t=T-T_c$, take $\alpha_2=at$ with $a>0$, and include the magnetic term $-Bm$. At $B=0$, minimizing the <Landau free energy> gives $m=0$ for $t>0$ and
$$
m^2=-\frac{\alpha_2}{\alpha_4}\propto(-t)
$$
for $t<0$, so the order-parameter <critical exponent> is $\beta=1/2$. Substitution gives a singular free-energy density proportional to $-t^2$ below $T_c$, whose second temperature derivative has a finite jump, so the heat-capacity exponent is $\alpha=0$. Above $T_c$, the equation $\alpha_2m+\alpha_4m^3=B$ gives the <magnetic susceptibility> $\chi=(\partial m/\partial B)_{B=0}=1/\alpha_2\sim t^{-1}$, hence $\gamma=1$. At $t=0$, $B=\alpha_4m^3$, so $m\sim B^{1/3}$ and $\delta=3$.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <Ginzburg criterion> compares fluctuations of the order parameter averaged over one correlation volume with the squared mean-field order parameter. The stated correlation function gives
$$
\langle(\delta m)^2\rangle_\xi
\sim\frac1{\xi^d}\int_0^\xi r^{d-1}r^{-(d-2)},dr
\sim\xi^{2-d}.
$$
Meanwhile $m_0^2\sim|t|\sim\xi^{-2}$, so their ratio scales as $\xi^{4-d}$. It vanishes at criticality for $d>4$, grows for $d<4$, and is marginal at $d=4$. Thus the ordinary scalar Landau-Ginzburg model has <upper critical dimension> $d_c=4$.

